      "Professional counseling did not emerge from one founder or one theory. It developed where career guidance, education, mental-health reform, psychological measurement, human development, and helping relationships began to overlap.",
      "## Frank Parsons and vocational guidance",
      "Frank Parsons is commonly associated with the beginning of the organized vocational-guidance movement and is often called the father of vocational guidance.",
      "The Vocation Bureau in Boston opened in 1908 with Parsons as director. His work, published in *Choosing a Vocation* in 1909, described a three-part approach:",
      "1. **Knowledge of self** — aptitudes, interests, abilities, ambitions, resources, and limitations\n2. **Knowledge of the world of work** — requirements, conditions, opportunities, advantages, and disadvantages of occupations\n3. **True reasoning** — matching knowledge of self with knowledge of occupational conditions",
      "This approach became the foundation for later **trait-and-factor** and person–environment-fit approaches.",
      "### Lasting professional contribution",
      "Parsons' contribution was not simply telling people which job to choose. He advanced a systematic process in which information about the person and information about work are connected through reasoned decision-making.",
      "## School guidance and Jesse B. Davis",
      "Jesse B. Davis is associated with the early development of systematic guidance in public schools. His work helped establish the idea that guidance could be integrated into education rather than offered only after a crisis or occupational failure.",
