      "## The five-part distinction",
      "None automatically substitutes for all the others.",
      "## State licensure",
      "Licensure is a legal credential. A state licensing board typically:",
      "- Reviews applications\n- Determines whether requirements are met\n- Issues licenses\n- Interprets and enforces applicable practice law and regulation\n- Investigates complaints within its authority\n- Disciplines licensees when warranted\n- Establishes renewal requirements",
      "Requirements vary and may include:",
      "- A qualifying graduate degree and coursework\n- Supervised clinical experience\n- One or more examinations\n- Background or jurisprudence requirements\n- Fees\n- Continuing education for renewal",
      "Passing an examination is generally one component of a licensure pathway, not the entire pathway.",
      "## National certification and the NCC",
      "NBCC's flagship credential is the **National Certified Counselor (NCC)**. National certification indicates that an individual has voluntarily met NBCC's certification standards and must comply with its ongoing requirements.",
      "The NCC:",
      "- Is a national professional certification.\n- Is not itself a state license.\n- Does not automatically authorize independent practice in a state.\n- Does not guarantee that a state board will grant a license.\n- May support professional identity and recognition.\n- Requires maintenance under NBCC rules.",
      "A counselor who holds the NCC must still comply with the law of every jurisdiction in which the counselor practices.",
      "## Programmatic accreditation",
      "CACREP accredits master's and doctoral counseling programs and specialized practice areas. Accreditation indicates that a program has been evaluated against professional standards through processes that include self-study and external review.",
      "CACREP accreditation:",
      "- Applies to the educational program, not automatically to every service later provided by a graduate.\n- Does not issue an individual counseling license.\n- Does not itself confer the NCC.\n- Can affect educational review, licensure eligibility, employment, or certification pathways depending on the applicable rules.\n- Must be verified for the program and relevant dates; institutional accreditation and programmatic accreditation are not the same.",
      "## The NCE's multiple uses",
      "The NCE may be used:",
      "- As an examination option in the NCC certification process\n- As part of state licensure requirements in many jurisdictions\n- For other recognized professional purposes described by the relevant authority",
      "The same examination result may be accepted in more than one pathway, but the candidate must separately satisfy each pathway's requirements.",
      "## Professional membership",
      "Joining ACA, a state counseling association, or a specialty organization can provide:",
      "- Professional community\n- Publications and education\n- Leadership opportunities\n- Advocacy\n- Specialty resources\n- Conferences and continuing education",
      "Membership is not a license. It does not create a protected professional title or independent-practice authority.",
      "## The Counseling Compact and privileges to practice",
      "The Counseling Compact is an interstate mechanism. In general, an eligible professional counselor uses an unencumbered independent home-state license to apply for a privilege to practice in an operational member state.",
      "### License versus privilege",
      "The privilege is not a national license. The counselor must follow the laws and rules applicable to practice in the remote state.",
      "## Credential-status language",
      "Counselors and applicants should represent credentials accurately.",
      "Examples:",
      "- `Passed the NCE` is not the same as `licensed counselor.`\n- `Graduated from a CACREP-accredited program` is not the same as `CACREP-certified counselor.`\n- `Applied for the NCC` is not the same as `NCC.`\n- `License eligible` is not the same as `licensed.`\n- A provisional or associate credential should not be presented as independent licensure.",
      "## Credential decision tree",
      "Ask:",
      "1. Is the question about legal authorization to practice? → **Licensure or compact privilege**\n2. Is it about an individual voluntarily meeting national professional standards? → **Certification/NCC**\n3. Is it about the quality and standards of a graduate counseling program? → **Accreditation/CACREP**\n4. Is it about measuring knowledge for a credentialing pathway? → **NCE or other examination**\n5. Is it about professional affiliation and resources? → **Membership/association**",
